{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RpCFfsYWzXdMGUb2NTjr8N","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed predicted experimental data for every \"planned\" experiment in their experiment plan, under the \"worst\" tier scenario. I need to produce a single YAML block with all the predicted results, rationales, and analysis for each planned experiment.\n\nLet me go through each planned experiment and generate worst-case predictions based on the worst tier definition:\n\n**Worst tier meaning:**\n- Per-DOF advantage does not transfer from MVP to real VLA\n- K=10 covariance too noisy in 7-DOF\n- CRM gain comes from residual learning, not conformal features\n- Key metric ranges provided\n\n\nNow I'm working through the specific experiments: plan_001 shows ACR-VLA on SmolVLA base with modest gains around 3% from residual learning rather than conformal features, and nominal degradation around 3%. Plan_002 is the critical ablation test where removing conformal features shows almost no performance gap—just ~1%—which would be the decisive result indicating conformal features contribute little to the overall CRM gains.\n\nThe remaining experiments show plan_003 finding per-DOF uncertainty performs similarly to scalar (~0% difference), plan_004 demonstrating SRPO stacking yields ~1% gain reaching 83% success rate, plan_005 validating COC signal with moderate Spearman correlation around 0.2 and ~5% ellipsoid narrowing, and plan_006 beginning to examine CRM performance without ACI.\n\nNow looking at the ablations: ACI alone contributes minimally since CRM is carrying the performance gains, while comparing different statistical correction methods (Bonferroni vs CGS) and COC signal types shows negligible differences. Interestingly, DiU's aleatoric uncertainty performs comparably or better than CGS epistemic uncertainty, suggesting the degradation in CGS is notable. I'm also starting to analyze feature importance through gradient magnitudes.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11303,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11303},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1e6879ea-cc71-427e-97a6-96f2664b189b","uuid":"eed13363-4a7d-44dd-8d42-7cf00ca47b80"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RpCFfsYWzXdMGUb2NTjr8N","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntier: worst\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  All three core claims fail their kill criteria. Per-DOF conformal advantage observed in MVP's\n  3-DOF synthetic setting does not transfer to 7-DOF flow-matching VLA: K=10 Ledoit-Wolf\n  covariance shrinks toward ~0.7×Identity, collapsing the 45D per-DOF features to near-spherical\n  noise indistinguishable from scalar uncertainty. CRM still provides modest gain (2-4pp) over\n  base VLA, but ablation A3 shows this is generic observation-conditioned residual learning —\n  conformal features contribute <2pp. COC Spearman ρ is weak (~0.2), ACI adaptation is\n  ineffective (volume narrowing <8%), and SRPO stacking is marginal (<2pp). Paper cannot\n  sustain the current three-claim structure. Reframe as workshop-length empirical analysis:\n  either (A) \"When Does Multivariate CP Help VLA Action Correction?\" diagnosing the K-vs-dimension\n  failure mode, or (B) \"Learned Residual Correction for VLAs\" if A3 baseline gain is robust.\n  Target CoRL/NeurIPS workshop or defer to ICLR 2027 with revised method (K≥30, diagonal-only,\n  or learned uncertainty embeddings).\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  # ============================================================\n  # plan_001: Full pipeline ACR-VLA on SmolVLA\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"ACR-VLA Full Pipeline (SmolVLA base)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_001\"\n    description: >\n      CGS + ACI/COC + CRM trained on LIBERO demos + synthetic augmentation.\n      Evaluate LIBERO nominal preservation and LIBERO-Plus L3-L5 robustness.\n    expected_results:\n      base_vla_liberoplus_sr_pct: 62.0\n      acr_vla_liberoplus_sr_pct: 65.1\n      delta_over_base_liberoplus_sr_pp: 3.1\n      base_vla_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 96.5\n      acr_vla_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 93.7\n      libero_nominal_degradation_pp: 2.8\n      cgs_avg_condition_number_7dof: 14.2\n      cgs_off_diagonal_avg_magnitude: 0.06\n      ledoit_wolf_shrinkage_intensity: 0.71\n      crm_train_loss_final: 0.0082\n      crm_val_loss_final: 0.0091\n      inference_overhead_ms_per_step: 38\n    result_rationale: >\n      CRM learns a modest observation-conditioned residual that helps on augmentable\n      perturbation types (camera jitter, Gaussian noise, robot-state offset) — these\n      overlap with synthetic training augmentations, so the residual network memorizes\n      correction patterns. The 3.1pp gain is real but comes almost entirely from the\n      residual learning pathway, not from conformal feature conditioning. Evidence:\n      Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage intensity is 0.71 (p/n = 10/7 ≈ 1.43 but effective p/n\n      after flow-matching sample correlation is ~0.7), meaning the 7×7 covariance\n      estimate is 71% identity matrix. Off-diagonal magnitudes average 0.06, meaning\n      cross-DOF covariance structure — the entire theoretical motivation — is washed\n      out. The 45D conformal feature vector is dominated by near-identical per-DOF\n      radii (since ellipsoid ≈ sphere) plus noise. Nominal degradation of 2.8pp is\n      concerning: CRM occasionally over-corrects on in-distribution data where the\n      base VLA was already correct, and the near-spherical conformal features provide\n      no signal to suppress correction.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 1: ACR-VLA vs Base VLA on LIBERO-Plus (L3-L5 avg)\"\n        rows:\n          - method: \"SmolVLA (base)\"\n            libero_nominal_sr: 96.5\n            liberoplus_avg_sr: 62.0\n            delta: \"—\"\n          - method: \"ACR-VLA (full)\"\n            libero_nominal_sr: 93.7\n            liberoplus_avg_sr: 65.1\n            delta: \"+3.1\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 1: Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage intensity vs dimensionality (3D MVP → 7D VLA). Shows sharp jump from 0.32 to 0.71 as DOF increases from 3 to 7 at K=10, with effective covariance collapsing toward identity.\"\n      - \"Fig 2: CRM training curves (train/val loss). Converges but val loss plateau suggests limited generalization beyond augmented perturbation types.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The pipeline technically works — CRM learns a useful residual — but the\n      mechanism is wrong. The 3.1pp gain is within the range where A3 ablation\n      (plan_002) will be decisive. The 0.71 shrinkage intensity is the smoking gun:\n      at this level, Ledoit-Wolf has replaced ~71% of the sample covariance with\n      identity, destroying the cross-DOF structure that is the paper's core\n      theoretical contribution. The condition number of 14.2 (vs ~5 in 3D MVP)\n      confirms numerical instability in the raw covariance that forces heavy\n      shrinkage.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # plan_002: Ablation A3 — CRM without conformal features\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"Ablation A3 — CRM without conformal features\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_002\"\n    description: >\n      CRM receives only observation embedding + base action, no 45D conformal\n      features. Equivalent to A2C2-style generic residual correction. MAKE-OR-BREAK\n      for Claim 1.\n    expected_results:\n      a3_liberoplus_sr_pct: 64.3\n      full_acr_vla_liberoplus_sr_pct: 65.1\n      a3_gap_full_vs_no_conformal_pp: 0.8\n      a3_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 94.5\n      a3_nominal_degradation_pp: 2.0\n      kill_criterion_triggered: true\n    result_rationale: >\n      The 0.8pp gap (65.1 vs 64.3) is well below the 2pp kill criterion. CRM's\n      residual correction capability comes from the observation embedding — it learns\n      \"this visual scene suggests the base VLA will err in X direction\" from the\n      synthetic augmentation training data. The 45D conformal features, being\n      near-spherical due to 0.71 shrinkage intensity, add only noise to this signal.\n      In fact, A3 shows slightly better nominal preservation (94.5 vs 93.7) because\n      removing the noisy conformal features reduces CRM's tendency to hallucinate\n      corrections when none are needed. This is the central failure: the per-DOF\n      conformal structure that worked in 3D (where shrinkage was 0.32 and off-diagonal\n      structure was preserved) is destroyed in 7D at K=10. The CRM has no useful\n      dimension-selective signal, so it falls back to learning a generic residual\n      from observation features.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2: A3 Ablation — Conformal Features Contribution\"\n        rows:\n          - method: \"SmolVLA (base)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 62.0\n            nominal_sr: 96.5\n          - method: \"A3: CRM no conformal\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 64.3\n            nominal_sr: 94.5\n          - method: \"ACR-VLA (full)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 65.1\n            nominal_sr: 93.7\n          - method: \"Gap (full - A3)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: \"+0.8\"\n            nominal_sr: \"-0.8\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 3: Bar chart comparing full ACR-VLA vs A3 across 7 perturbation types. Error bars overlap on all 7 dimensions, confirming no perturbation type where conformal features provide statistically significant advantage.\"\n    analysis: >\n      CLAIM 1 IS KILLED. The 0.8pp gap is not statistically significant given\n      typical LIBERO-Plus variance (~1.5pp between seeds). The conformal features\n      are noise in 7-DOF at K=10. The observation-conditioned residual does useful\n      work, but this is not a novel contribution — A2C2 and PLD already demonstrate\n      learned residual correction. The paper's core differentiator (per-DOF\n      conformal features as CRM input) is invalidated.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # plan_003: Ablation A4 — scalar uncertainty replacing per-DOF\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"Ablation A4 — CRM with scalar uncertainty\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_003\"\n    description: >\n      CRM with scalar uncertainty (Euclidean distance à la ReconVLA) replacing\n      45D per-DOF features. Tests per-DOF vs scalar.\n    expected_results:\n      a4_liberoplus_sr_pct: 65.3\n      full_acr_vla_liberoplus_sr_pct: 65.1\n      a4_gap_perDOF_vs_scalar_pp: -0.2\n      a4_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 94.1\n    result_rationale: >\n      Scalar uncertainty (single Euclidean distance from K=10 samples) actually\n      performs on par or marginally better than the 45D per-DOF features. This is\n      because in the worst case, the per-DOF features are near-spherical noise:\n      all 7 per-DOF conformal radii are nearly identical (ratio of max to min\n      radius ≈ 1.08), the covariance-derived features carry no cross-DOF signal,\n      and the 45D input dimensionality adds learnable parameters that overfit to\n      training augmentation patterns. The scalar distance, being lower-dimensional,\n      is more robust. The -0.2pp \"advantage\" of scalar over per-DOF is within\n      noise, but the direction is damning: the 42 additional per-DOF dimensions\n      provide zero marginal information. This directly contradicts the paper's core\n      insight that \"per-DOF conformal statistics encode which dimensions are\n      uncertain and how they co-vary.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 3: A4 Ablation — Per-DOF vs Scalar Uncertainty\"\n        rows:\n          - method: \"A3: no uncertainty\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 64.3\n            uncertainty_dim: 0\n          - method: \"A4: scalar (Euclidean)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 65.3\n            uncertainty_dim: 1\n          - method: \"ACR-VLA: per-DOF (45D)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 65.1\n            uncertainty_dim: 45\n          - method: \"Gap (per-DOF - scalar)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: \"-0.2\"\n            uncertainty_dim: \"—\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 4: Scatter plot of per-DOF conformal radii across 7 DOFs for 500 random steps. Near-circular cloud confirms ellipsoid collapse to sphere. Overlay: same plot from MVP 3D experiment showing clear elliptical structure.\"\n    analysis: >\n      CLAIM 1 IS DOUBLY KILLED. Not only do conformal features barely help over\n      no-uncertainty (A3), but per-DOF features don't beat scalar. The 45D→1D\n      compression loses nothing because there was nothing to lose: K=10 in 7-DOF\n      produces near-isotropic uncertainty estimates. The theoretical advantage of\n      multivariate CP (encoding cross-dimensional covariance) requires sufficient\n      samples relative to dimensionality, and K=10 with 7 DOFs is below the\n      practical threshold.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # plan_004: SRPO stacking\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"ACR-VLA stacked on SRPO\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_004\"\n    description: >\n      Train new CRM on SRPO-finetuned VLA checkpoint. Evaluate LIBERO-Plus.\n      Tests orthogonal complementarity claim.\n    expected_results:\n      srpo_baseline_liberoplus_sr_pct: 82.1\n      srpo_plus_acr_liberoplus_sr_pct: 83.4\n      srpo_stacking_gain_pp: 1.3\n      srpo_plus_acr_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 95.8\n      srpo_nominal_sr_pct: 97.2\n      srpo_nominal_degradation_pp: 1.4\n    result_rationale: >\n      SRPO at 82.1% has already corrected most of the failure modes that CRM's\n      generic residual learning can address. The remaining ~18% failures are\n      dominated by layout and language perturbations — distribution shifts that\n      (a) synthetic augmentation cannot cover, and (b) K=10 conformal statistics\n      cannot distinguish from in-distribution because flow-matching samples lack\n      diversity under these novel conditions. The 1.3pp stacking gain is below\n      the 2pp kill criterion. Moreover, the gain is not statistically robust:\n      SRPO's higher base performance means the failure cases are harder, more\n      diverse, and less amenable to a fixed residual correction pattern. The\n      1.4pp nominal degradation is proportionally less concerning (97.2→95.8)\n      but still present, suggesting CRM interferes with SRPO's already-good\n      action predictions on a nontrivial fraction of in-distribution steps.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 4: SRPO Stacking\"\n        rows:\n          - method: \"SRPO (training-time SOTA)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 82.1\n            libero_nominal_sr: 97.2\n          - method: \"SRPO + ACR-VLA\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 83.4\n            libero_nominal_sr: 95.8\n          - method: \"Stacking gain\"\n            liberoplus_sr: \"+1.3\"\n            libero_nominal_sr: \"-1.4\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 5: Per-perturbation stacking gain on top of SRPO. Only camera_noise and robot_state show >1pp gain; layout and language show 0 or negative.\"\n    analysis: >\n      CLAIM 3 IS KILLED. The 1.3pp stacking gain fails the 2pp criterion and is\n      likely not statistically significant across seeds. The orthogonal\n      complementarity argument is undermined: ACR-VLA's residual correction\n      overlaps with what SRPO already learned during training. The remaining\n      failure modes (layout, language) require semantic understanding beyond\n      what observation-conditioned residuals or conformal statistics can provide.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # plan_005: COC signal validation\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"COC Signal Validation\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_005\"\n    description: >\n      (a) Spearman ρ between COC coverage fraction and task success,\n      (b) ACI convergence, (c) ellipsoid volume comparison vs static.\n    expected_results:\n      coc_success_spearman_rho: 0.19\n      coc_success_spearman_p_value: 0.04\n      aci_convergence_episodes_to_stable: 45\n      aci_final_alpha_mean: 0.12\n      aci_final_alpha_std: 0.04\n      ellipsoid_volume_narrowing_pct: 6.2\n      static_calibration_volume_mean_log: 3.8\n      aci_adapted_volume_mean_log: 3.56\n    result_rationale: >\n      COC Spearman ρ = 0.19 is positive but below the 0.3 threshold needed for\n      a reliable ACI signal. The fundamental problem: COC measures consistency\n      between overlapping chunk predictions, but flow-matching VLAs generate\n      each chunk from a stochastic denoising process. At K=10, the inter-chunk\n      consistency is dominated by denoising noise rather than epistemic\n      uncertainty about the task. Two consecutive chunks may disagree not because\n      the policy is uncertain but because the diffusion sampling paths diverge.\n      This makes COC a noisy proxy for success — it detects catastrophic\n      failures (ρ > 0 because complete policy collapse does produce inconsistent\n      chunks) but cannot distinguish moderate uncertainty from sampling noise.\n      ACI converges (45 episodes) but converges to a threshold that produces\n      only 6.2% volume narrowing vs static — the adaptation is technically\n      functional but practically useless, as the conformal regions barely\n      change shape. The ellipsoid volume in log-space shifts from 3.8 to 3.56,\n      a negligible tightening that CRM cannot meaningfully exploit.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 5: COC Signal Quality\"\n        rows:\n          - metric: \"Spearman ρ (COC vs success)\"\n            value: 0.19\n            threshold: \"≥0.3\"\n            status: \"FAIL\"\n          - metric: \"Ellipsoid volume narrowing\"\n            value: \"6.2%\"\n            threshold: \"≥10%\"\n            status: \"FAIL\"\n          - metric: \"ACI convergence episodes\"\n            value: 45\n            threshold: \"—\"\n            status: \"—\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 6: Scatter plot of per-episode COC coverage fraction vs binary task success. Weak positive trend visible but high overlap between success/failure distributions. ρ = 0.19.\"\n      - \"Fig 7: ACI α_t trajectory over 100 episodes. Converges around episode 45 but oscillates ±0.04, indicating the COC signal is too noisy for stable adaptation.\"\n      - \"Fig 8: Ellipsoid volume (log scale) under static vs ACI-adapted calibration. Distributions largely overlap; ACI shifts the mean by 0.24 log-units (6.2% linear narrowing).\"\n    analysis: >\n      CLAIM 2 IS KILLED (both sub-criteria fail). COC is too noisy as an\n      ACI signal in the flow-matching setting. The self-referential per-step\n      coverage proxy — the key innovation enabling unsupervised online\n      adaptation — does not provide sufficient signal-to-noise ratio when\n      the base policy's stochasticity dominates inter-chunk disagreement.\n      The ACI mechanism is mathematically sound (it does converge) but\n      converges to a near-static threshold because the noisy COC signal\n      averages out to roughly the marginal coverage rate.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # plan_006: Ablation A1 — CRM without ACI\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"Ablation A1 — CRM without ACI (static calibration)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_006\"\n    description: >\n      CRM with static CGS calibration, no online ACI adaptation.\n      Isolates ACI contribution.\n    expected_results:\n      a1_liberoplus_sr_pct: 64.7\n      full_acr_vla_liberoplus_sr_pct: 65.1\n      a1_gap_pp: 0.4\n      a1_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 93.9\n    result_rationale: >\n      ACI's contribution is negligible (0.4pp) because, as plan_005 shows, the\n      COC-driven adaptation produces only 6.2% volume narrowing. The CRM input\n      features under static vs ACI-adapted calibration are nearly identical —\n      the conformal radii shift by <7% on average, well within the noise floor\n      that CRM has already learned to ignore. This confirms that in the worst\n      case, ACI is a dead module: it runs, it converges, but the adapted\n      conformal regions are functionally identical to static ones. The entire\n      online adaptation pathway (COC → ACI → updated conformal threshold →\n      modified CGS features → CRM) is a no-op in practice.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 6: A1 Ablation — ACI Contribution\"\n        rows:\n          - method: \"A1: CRM + static CGS\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 64.7\n            nominal_sr: 93.9\n          - method: \"ACR-VLA (full, with ACI)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 65.1\n            nominal_sr: 93.7\n          - method: \"ACI contribution\"\n            liberoplus_sr: \"+0.4\"\n            nominal_sr: \"-0.2\"\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      ACI adds nothing. The entire online adaptation subsystem — arguably the\n      most theoretically elegant component — is inert because its input signal\n      (COC) is too noisy and its output effect (volume narrowing) is too small.\n      This eliminates Claim 2's practical value entirely.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # plan_007: Ablation A2 — ACI without CRM\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"Ablation A2 — ACI without CRM (sample selection only)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_007\"\n    description: >\n      CGS + COC + sample selection only, extending ReconVLA to multivariate.\n      No learned residual correction. Isolates CRM contribution.\n    expected_results:\n      a2_liberoplus_sr_pct: 62.9\n      full_acr_vla_liberoplus_sr_pct: 65.1\n      a2_vs_base_gain_pp: 0.9\n      a2_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 96.1\n      reconvla_scalar_selection_liberoplus_sr_pct: 63.2\n    result_rationale: >\n      Without CRM, the system reduces to \"multivariate ReconVLA\" — selecting\n      among K=10 samples based on conformal region membership rather than\n      Euclidean distance. The 0.9pp gain over base is marginal and actually\n      slightly worse than scalar ReconVLA-style selection (63.2%), because the\n      near-spherical conformal regions at K=10 in 7-DOF make the multivariate\n      selection criterion equivalent to (but noisier than) simple Euclidean\n      distance. The selection approach is fundamentally limited: it can only\n      choose from K=10 existing samples, and at this small K the best sample\n      is often close to the mean regardless of selection criterion. CRM's\n      ability to synthesize a correction beyond any individual sample is what\n      drives the ~3pp full pipeline gain — confirming that the residual\n      learning, not the conformal machinery, is the active ingredient.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 7: A2 Ablation — CRM vs Sample Selection\"\n        rows:\n          - method: \"SmolVLA (base)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 62.0\n          - method: \"A2: multivariate selection (no CRM)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 62.9\n          - method: \"ReconVLA-style scalar selection\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 63.2\n          - method: \"ACR-VLA (full)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 65.1\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 9: CDF of action error improvement. Sample selection (A2) shifts CDF marginally; CRM (full) produces a larger but still modest shift. The CRM shift is similar to A3 (no conformal), confirming the residual mechanism.\"\n    analysis: >\n      CRM is doing the heavy lifting (65.1 vs 62.9 without it). But as A3\n      shows, CRM doesn't need conformal features. The multivariate sample\n      selection is slightly worse than scalar selection, a particularly\n      damning result for the paper's core thesis.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # plan_008: Per-perturbation breakdown\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"Per-Perturbation Breakdown\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_008\"\n    description: >\n      ACR-VLA gain across 7 LIBERO-Plus perturbation dimensions.\n    expected_results:\n      per_perturbation_gain_pp:\n        camera_noise: 4.8\n        gaussian_noise: 4.2\n        robot_state_offset: 3.5\n        lighting_change: 2.1\n        background_change: 1.5\n        layout_change: 0.3\n        language_rephrase: -0.2\n      avg_augmentable_gain_pp: 4.17\n      avg_non_augmentable_gain_pp: 0.53\n    result_rationale: >\n      The gain pattern exposes the mechanism: ACR-VLA helps most on perturbation\n      types covered by synthetic augmentation (camera noise, Gaussian noise,\n      robot-state offset — avg 4.17pp). These are precisely the perturbations\n      where the CRM's observation-conditioned residual has seen training examples.\n      Non-augmentable perturbations (lighting, background, layout, language)\n      show minimal or zero gain because (a) the residual network has no training\n      signal for these shifts, and (b) K=10 conformal statistics are equally\n      uninformative for novel vs familiar perturbations (the near-spherical\n      uncertainty regions don't differentially respond to distribution shift\n      type). Language rephrase shows -0.2pp because the CRM occasionally\n      applies a correction to steps where the base VLA would have been correct\n      under the rephrased instruction, and the conformal features provide no\n      signal to suppress this spurious correction. The 4-5pp gains on\n      augmentable perturbations are genuine but attributable to residual\n      learning (see A3), not conformal features.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 8: Per-Perturbation ACR-VLA Gain\"\n        rows:\n          - perturbation: \"Camera noise\"\n            base_sr: 55.2\n            acr_sr: 60.0\n            gain_pp: \"+4.8\"\n          - perturbation: \"Gaussian noise\"\n            base_sr: 58.1\n            acr_sr: 62.3\n            gain_pp: \"+4.2\"\n          - perturbation: \"Robot-state offset\"\n            base_sr: 51.8\n            acr_sr: 55.3\n            gain_pp: \"+3.5\"\n          - perturbation: \"Lighting\"\n            base_sr: 68.3\n            acr_sr: 70.4\n            gain_pp: \"+2.1\"\n          - perturbation: \"Background\"\n            base_sr: 71.0\n            acr_sr: 72.5\n            gain_pp: \"+1.5\"\n          - perturbation: \"Layout\"\n            base_sr: 60.5\n            acr_sr: 60.8\n            gain_pp: \"+0.3\"\n          - perturbation: \"Language\"\n            base_sr: 69.1\n            acr_sr: 68.9\n            gain_pp: \"-0.2\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 10: Grouped bar chart of per-perturbation gain. Clear divide between augmentable (green, >3pp) and non-augmentable (gray, <2pp) perturbation types. Annotated with A3 per-perturbation comparison showing near-identical pattern.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The perturbation breakdown is the most informative diagnostic. It reveals\n      that ACR-VLA's gains are entirely driven by synthetic augmentation coverage,\n      not by conformal uncertainty adaptation. A truly uncertainty-aware system\n      should help on novel perturbations (where uncertainty is highest) — instead,\n      gains correlate with augmentation coverage (r > 0.9). This is the signature\n      of a memorized residual, not an adaptive uncertainty-conditioned correction.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # plan_009: Ablation A5 — CGS vs Bonferroni\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"Ablation A5 — CGS vs per-DOF Bonferroni\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_009\"\n    description: >\n      Joint Mahalanobis (CGS) vs per-DOF Bonferroni correction.\n      Tests whether multivariate CP outperforms independent marginal CP.\n    expected_results:\n      a5_cgs_liberoplus_sr_pct: 65.1\n      a5_bonferroni_liberoplus_sr_pct: 65.0\n      a5_gap_pp: 0.1\n      bonferroni_ellipsoid_volume_ratio_vs_cgs: 1.03\n    result_rationale: >\n      Since the K=10 Ledoit-Wolf covariance is 71% identity, CGS's joint\n      Mahalanobis ellipsoid is nearly a sphere — functionally equivalent to\n      independent per-DOF intervals with Bonferroni correction. The 0.1pp\n      difference is pure noise. The theoretical advantage of CGS (capturing\n      cross-DOF covariance to produce tighter ellipsoids aligned with the\n      actual uncertainty structure) requires off-diagonal covariance elements\n      that are statistically significant, which K=10 in 7-DOF cannot provide.\n      The Bonferroni rectangular region and CGS near-spherical region have\n      similar volumes (ratio 1.03) and contain essentially the same samples,\n      producing identical CRM inputs in practice.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 9: A5 Ablation — CGS vs Bonferroni\"\n        rows:\n          - method: \"CGS (joint Mahalanobis)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 65.1\n            volume_log: 3.56\n          - method: \"Per-DOF Bonferroni\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 65.0\n            volume_log: 3.67\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 11: 2D projection (DOF 1 vs DOF 2) of CGS ellipsoid vs Bonferroni rectangle for 100 random steps. Ellipsoid is visually circular, nearly inscribed in the rectangle. Cross-DOF tilt angle < 3° from axes.\"\n    analysis: >\n      CGS's multivariate CP offers no advantage over naïve Bonferroni. This is\n      a direct consequence of the covariance collapse at K=10. In the MVP 3D\n      setting, CGS showed clear elliptical tilt (15-25° off-axis), demonstrating\n      genuine cross-DOF structure. At 7-DOF with K=10, this structure vanishes.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # plan_010: Ablation A6 — COC vs episode-level ACI\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"Ablation A6 — COC vs episode-level task success for ACI\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_010\"\n    description: >\n      ACI updated with episode-level binary success signal instead of\n      per-step COC. Tests self-referential signal value.\n    expected_results:\n      a6_episode_aci_liberoplus_sr_pct: 65.0\n      coc_aci_liberoplus_sr_pct: 65.1\n      a6_gap_pp: -0.1\n      episode_aci_convergence_episodes: 30\n      episode_aci_volume_narrowing_pct: 7.8\n    result_rationale: >\n      Episode-level ACI actually converges faster (30 vs 45 episodes) and\n      produces slightly more volume narrowing (7.8% vs 6.2%) because the\n      binary success signal, while sparser, is lower-noise than COC. COC's\n      per-step granularity is theoretically valuable but in practice the\n      noise from flow-matching stochasticity drowns the signal advantage.\n      The -0.1pp \"gap\" (episode ACI marginally better) further undermines\n      Claim 2: COC's self-referential per-step mechanism — the paper's\n      claimed innovation for unsupervised online adaptation — performs no\n      better than the simpler episode-level signal. Neither signal produces\n      meaningful ACI adaptation at this operating point, so the comparison\n      is between two flavors of ineffective.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 10: A6 Ablation — COC vs Episode-level ACI\"\n        rows:\n          - signal: \"COC (per-step, self-referential)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 65.1\n            convergence_episodes: 45\n            volume_narrowing_pct: 6.2\n          - signal: \"Episode success (binary)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 65.0\n            convergence_episodes: 30\n            volume_narrowing_pct: 7.8\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 12: Overlay of ACI α_t trajectories for COC-driven vs episode-driven adaptation. Episode signal is smoother, converges earlier, but both plateau at similar α values.\"\n    analysis: >\n      COC provides no advantage over episode-level signal. The per-step\n      self-referential innovation adds complexity without benefit. For the\n      reframed paper, this is a useful negative result: it characterizes\n      the signal-to-noise boundary where per-step conformal consistency\n      breaks down.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # plan_011: Ablation A7 — DiU aleatoric vs CGS epistemic\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"Ablation A7 — DiU aleatoric vs CGS epistemic features\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_011\"\n    description: >\n      CRM with DiU aleatoric uncertainty features (7D) replacing CGS\n      epistemic features (45D). Also test combined 52D input.\n    expected_results:\n      a7_diu_only_liberoplus_sr_pct: 64.9\n      a7_combined_52d_liberoplus_sr_pct: 65.4\n      full_cgs_only_liberoplus_sr_pct: 65.1\n      a3_no_uncertainty_liberoplus_sr_pct: 64.3\n      diu_vs_cgs_gap_pp: -0.2\n      combined_vs_cgs_gap_pp: 0.3\n    result_rationale: >\n      DiU's 7D aleatoric features (per-DOF variance from single-pass\n      estimation) perform comparably to CGS's 45D epistemic features\n      (64.9 vs 65.1, gap -0.2pp within noise). This is because in the\n      worst case, both are providing low-quality uncertainty signals: CGS\n      is near-spherical noise, and DiU captures aleatoric variance that\n      is present in-distribution and out-of-distribution alike (not\n      discriminative for perturbation detection). The combined 52D input\n      shows a marginal 0.3pp improvement, which is likely from the slight\n      orthogonal information in DiU's per-sample variance vs CGS's\n      inter-sample spread, but is not significant. The key takeaway: when\n      the CGS features are degraded (as in worst case), replacing them\n      with a simpler uncertainty source makes no difference because the\n      CRM is already ignoring uncertainty features in favor of observation\n      conditioning. All uncertainty variants cluster within 1.1pp of each\n      other, while all are only ~1pp above the no-uncertainty A3 baseline.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 11: A7 Ablation — Uncertainty Feature Source\"\n        rows:\n          - features: \"None (A3)\"\n            dim: 0\n            liberoplus_sr: 64.3\n          - features: \"DiU aleatoric\"\n            dim: 7\n            liberoplus_sr: 64.9\n          - features: \"CGS epistemic (full)\"\n            dim: 45\n            liberoplus_sr: 65.1\n          - features: \"Combined\"\n            dim: 52\n            liberoplus_sr: 65.4\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 13: Feature ablation ladder. All uncertainty variants within 1.1pp band; observation-conditioned residual (A3 at 64.3) provides the bulk of improvement over base (62.0).\"\n    analysis: >\n      The epistemic-vs-aleatoric distinction is moot when both signals are\n      degraded. CGS's theoretical advantage (epistemic uncertainty rises\n      under distribution shift) is lost because the covariance estimation\n      noise masks the epistemic signal. DiU's aleatoric features are at\n      least lower-dimensional and don't carry covariance estimation noise,\n      making them a simpler alternative that performs identically.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # plan_012: Feature importance analysis\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"CRM Feature Importance Analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_012\"\n    description: >\n      Gradient magnitude of CRM output w.r.t. 45 uncertainty features,\n      broken down by perturbation type.\n    expected_results:\n      avg_gradient_magnitude_observation_features: 0.042\n      avg_gradient_magnitude_conformal_features: 0.008\n      observation_to_conformal_gradient_ratio: 5.25\n      top5_features_by_gradient: [\"obs_embed_dim_127\", \"obs_embed_dim_43\", \"obs_embed_dim_201\", \"base_action_dof_1\", \"base_action_dof_3\"]\n      conformal_feature_gradient_std_across_perturbations: 0.002\n      max_conformal_feature_gradient_any_perturbation: 0.014\n    result_rationale: >\n      Gradient analysis reveals that CRM overwhelmingly attends to observation\n      embedding dimensions (avg gradient magnitude 0.042) over conformal\n      features (0.008) — a 5.25× ratio. The top 5 features by gradient\n      magnitude are all observation embedding or base action dimensions; no\n      conformal feature appears in the top 20. Furthermore, conformal feature\n      gradients show near-zero variance across perturbation types (std 0.002),\n      meaning CRM does not differentially use conformal features for different\n      perturbation types — it has learned to effectively ignore them. The\n      maximum gradient magnitude for any conformal feature under any\n      perturbation (0.014) is still 3× lower than the average observation\n      feature gradient. This is the mechanistic explanation for A3's result:\n      CRM's learned weights suppress the noisy conformal features and rely\n      on observation conditioning for correction decisions.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 12: Feature Importance — Gradient Magnitude\"\n        rows:\n          - feature_group: \"Observation embedding (128D)\"\n            avg_gradient: 0.042\n            pct_of_total: \"78.5%\"\n          - feature_group: \"Base action (7D)\"\n            avg_gradient: 0.031\n            pct_of_total: \"14.2%\"\n          - feature_group: \"Conformal per-DOF std (7D)\"\n            avg_gradient: 0.009\n            pct_of_total: \"3.1%\"\n          - feature_group: \"Conformal radii (7D)\"\n            avg_gradient: 0.008\n            pct_of_total: \"2.8%\"\n          - feature_group: \"Ellipsoid volume (1D)\"\n            avg_gradient: 0.007\n            pct_of_total: \"0.4%\"\n          - feature_group: \"Cross-DOF covariance (21D + 2D)\"\n            avg_gradient: 0.006\n            pct_of_total: \"1.0%\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 14: Heatmap of gradient magnitudes (45 conformal features × 7 perturbation types). Near-uniform low magnitude across all cells — no perturbation-specific conformal feature usage pattern.\"\n      - \"Fig 15: Bar chart comparing observation vs conformal feature gradient magnitudes. 5.25× ratio clearly visible.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The gradient analysis provides the mechanistic autopsy of Claim 1's\n      failure. CRM learned to ignore conformal features because they are\n      uninformative (near-spherical, perturbation-agnostic). The network\n      allocated its limited capacity to observation features, which do carry\n      perturbation-discriminative information. This is rational behavior by\n      the optimizer — it confirms the features are genuinely useless, not\n      that the architecture is wrong.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # plan_013: SnapFlow integration\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"SnapFlow K=10 1-step Distilled Generation\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_013\"\n    description: >\n      K=10 samples via 1-step distilled flow matching (SnapFlow).\n      Test sample diversity and inference speed.\n    expected_results:\n      snapflow_inference_time_ms: 12\n      standard_10step_inference_time_ms: 38\n      speedup_factor: 3.17\n      snapflow_sample_diversity_avg_std_per_dof: 0.018\n      standard_sample_diversity_avg_std_per_dof: 0.031\n      diversity_reduction_pct: 42\n      snapflow_acr_liberoplus_sr_pct: 63.8\n      standard_acr_liberoplus_sr_pct: 65.1\n      snapflow_degradation_pp: 1.3\n    result_rationale: >\n      SnapFlow's 1-step distilled generation produces K=10 samples 3.17×\n      faster but with 42% less diversity (per-DOF std drops from 0.031 to\n      0.018). The diversity reduction directly impacts conformal statistics:\n      the already-noisy K=10 covariance becomes even more concentrated,\n      with shrinkage intensity increasing from 0.71 to ~0.82. The 1.3pp\n      performance drop (65.1→63.8) is modest because, as established,\n      conformal features contribute little — the degradation comes from\n      reduced sample diversity slightly weakening the mean-action estimate\n      that CRM uses as its base. The 3.17× speedup is the only unambiguous\n      positive result, but it's a SnapFlow contribution, not an ACR-VLA\n      contribution. For the reframed paper, this could be cited as evidence\n      that the pipeline is practical at inference time, but it doesn't save\n      the conformal features story.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 13: SnapFlow Integration\"\n        rows:\n          - method: \"ACR-VLA (10-step sampling)\"\n            inference_ms: 38\n            liberoplus_sr: 65.1\n            sample_diversity: 0.031\n          - method: \"ACR-VLA + SnapFlow (1-step)\"\n            inference_ms: 12\n            liberoplus_sr: 63.8\n            sample_diversity: 0.018\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 16: Sample diversity (per-DOF std) histogram for 10-step vs SnapFlow 1-step. SnapFlow distribution is sharply concentrated near zero, further compressing the covariance estimate.\"\n    analysis: >\n      SnapFlow trades diversity for speed. In the worst case, this further\n      degrades already-insufficient conformal features. The practical\n      takeaway: if conformal features are noise anyway, SnapFlow's speedup\n      is free — the system loses nothing by using faster, less diverse\n      samples because the conformal pathway was already inert.\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11303,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11303},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1e6879ea-cc71-427e-97a6-96f2664b189b","uuid":"1d2c7e8a-7206-410e-bb19-1317b34f2e79"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":227390,"duration_api_ms":228677,"num_turns":1,"result":"```yaml\ntier: worst\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  All three core claims fail their kill criteria. Per-DOF conformal advantage observed in MVP's\n  3-DOF synthetic setting does not transfer to 7-DOF flow-matching VLA: K=10 Ledoit-Wolf\n  covariance shrinks toward ~0.7×Identity, collapsing the 45D per-DOF features to near-spherical\n  noise indistinguishable from scalar uncertainty. CRM still provides modest gain (2-4pp) over\n  base VLA, but ablation A3 shows this is generic observation-conditioned residual learning —\n  conformal features contribute <2pp. COC Spearman ρ is weak (~0.2), ACI adaptation is\n  ineffective (volume narrowing <8%), and SRPO stacking is marginal (<2pp). Paper cannot\n  sustain the current three-claim structure. Reframe as workshop-length empirical analysis:\n  either (A) \"When Does Multivariate CP Help VLA Action Correction?\" diagnosing the K-vs-dimension\n  failure mode, or (B) \"Learned Residual Correction for VLAs\" if A3 baseline gain is robust.\n  Target CoRL/NeurIPS workshop or defer to ICLR 2027 with revised method (K≥30, diagonal-only,\n  or learned uncertainty embeddings).\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  # ============================================================\n  # plan_001: Full pipeline ACR-VLA on SmolVLA\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"ACR-VLA Full Pipeline (SmolVLA base)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_001\"\n    description: >\n      CGS + ACI/COC + CRM trained on LIBERO demos + synthetic augmentation.\n      Evaluate LIBERO nominal preservation and LIBERO-Plus L3-L5 robustness.\n    expected_results:\n      base_vla_liberoplus_sr_pct: 62.0\n      acr_vla_liberoplus_sr_pct: 65.1\n      delta_over_base_liberoplus_sr_pp: 3.1\n      base_vla_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 96.5\n      acr_vla_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 93.7\n      libero_nominal_degradation_pp: 2.8\n      cgs_avg_condition_number_7dof: 14.2\n      cgs_off_diagonal_avg_magnitude: 0.06\n      ledoit_wolf_shrinkage_intensity: 0.71\n      crm_train_loss_final: 0.0082\n      crm_val_loss_final: 0.0091\n      inference_overhead_ms_per_step: 38\n    result_rationale: >\n      CRM learns a modest observation-conditioned residual that helps on augmentable\n      perturbation types (camera jitter, Gaussian noise, robot-state offset) — these\n      overlap with synthetic training augmentations, so the residual network memorizes\n      correction patterns. The 3.1pp gain is real but comes almost entirely from the\n      residual learning pathway, not from conformal feature conditioning. Evidence:\n      Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage intensity is 0.71 (p/n = 10/7 ≈ 1.43 but effective p/n\n      after flow-matching sample correlation is ~0.7), meaning the 7×7 covariance\n      estimate is 71% identity matrix. Off-diagonal magnitudes average 0.06, meaning\n      cross-DOF covariance structure — the entire theoretical motivation — is washed\n      out. The 45D conformal feature vector is dominated by near-identical per-DOF\n      radii (since ellipsoid ≈ sphere) plus noise. Nominal degradation of 2.8pp is\n      concerning: CRM occasionally over-corrects on in-distribution data where the\n      base VLA was already correct, and the near-spherical conformal features provide\n      no signal to suppress correction.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 1: ACR-VLA vs Base VLA on LIBERO-Plus (L3-L5 avg)\"\n        rows:\n          - method: \"SmolVLA (base)\"\n            libero_nominal_sr: 96.5\n            liberoplus_avg_sr: 62.0\n            delta: \"—\"\n          - method: \"ACR-VLA (full)\"\n            libero_nominal_sr: 93.7\n            liberoplus_avg_sr: 65.1\n            delta: \"+3.1\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 1: Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage intensity vs dimensionality (3D MVP → 7D VLA). Shows sharp jump from 0.32 to 0.71 as DOF increases from 3 to 7 at K=10, with effective covariance collapsing toward identity.\"\n      - \"Fig 2: CRM training curves (train/val loss). Converges but val loss plateau suggests limited generalization beyond augmented perturbation types.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The pipeline technically works — CRM learns a useful residual — but the\n      mechanism is wrong. The 3.1pp gain is within the range where A3 ablation\n      (plan_002) will be decisive. The 0.71 shrinkage intensity is the smoking gun:\n      at this level, Ledoit-Wolf has replaced ~71% of the sample covariance with\n      identity, destroying the cross-DOF structure that is the paper's core\n      theoretical contribution. The condition number of 14.2 (vs ~5 in 3D MVP)\n      confirms numerical instability in the raw covariance that forces heavy\n      shrinkage.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # plan_002: Ablation A3 — CRM without conformal features\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"Ablation A3 — CRM without conformal features\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_002\"\n    description: >\n      CRM receives only observation embedding + base action, no 45D conformal\n      features. Equivalent to A2C2-style generic residual correction. MAKE-OR-BREAK\n      for Claim 1.\n    expected_results:\n      a3_liberoplus_sr_pct: 64.3\n      full_acr_vla_liberoplus_sr_pct: 65.1\n      a3_gap_full_vs_no_conformal_pp: 0.8\n      a3_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 94.5\n      a3_nominal_degradation_pp: 2.0\n      kill_criterion_triggered: true\n    result_rationale: >\n      The 0.8pp gap (65.1 vs 64.3) is well below the 2pp kill criterion. CRM's\n      residual correction capability comes from the observation embedding — it learns\n      \"this visual scene suggests the base VLA will err in X direction\" from the\n      synthetic augmentation training data. The 45D conformal features, being\n      near-spherical due to 0.71 shrinkage intensity, add only noise to this signal.\n      In fact, A3 shows slightly better nominal preservation (94.5 vs 93.7) because\n      removing the noisy conformal features reduces CRM's tendency to hallucinate\n      corrections when none are needed. This is the central failure: the per-DOF\n      conformal structure that worked in 3D (where shrinkage was 0.32 and off-diagonal\n      structure was preserved) is destroyed in 7D at K=10. The CRM has no useful\n      dimension-selective signal, so it falls back to learning a generic residual\n      from observation features.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2: A3 Ablation — Conformal Features Contribution\"\n        rows:\n          - method: \"SmolVLA (base)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 62.0\n            nominal_sr: 96.5\n          - method: \"A3: CRM no conformal\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 64.3\n            nominal_sr: 94.5\n          - method: \"ACR-VLA (full)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 65.1\n            nominal_sr: 93.7\n          - method: \"Gap (full - A3)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: \"+0.8\"\n            nominal_sr: \"-0.8\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 3: Bar chart comparing full ACR-VLA vs A3 across 7 perturbation types. Error bars overlap on all 7 dimensions, confirming no perturbation type where conformal features provide statistically significant advantage.\"\n    analysis: >\n      CLAIM 1 IS KILLED. The 0.8pp gap is not statistically significant given\n      typical LIBERO-Plus variance (~1.5pp between seeds). The conformal features\n      are noise in 7-DOF at K=10. The observation-conditioned residual does useful\n      work, but this is not a novel contribution — A2C2 and PLD already demonstrate\n      learned residual correction. The paper's core differentiator (per-DOF\n      conformal features as CRM input) is invalidated.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # plan_003: Ablation A4 — scalar uncertainty replacing per-DOF\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"Ablation A4 — CRM with scalar uncertainty\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_003\"\n    description: >\n      CRM with scalar uncertainty (Euclidean distance à la ReconVLA) replacing\n      45D per-DOF features. Tests per-DOF vs scalar.\n    expected_results:\n      a4_liberoplus_sr_pct: 65.3\n      full_acr_vla_liberoplus_sr_pct: 65.1\n      a4_gap_perDOF_vs_scalar_pp: -0.2\n      a4_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 94.1\n    result_rationale: >\n      Scalar uncertainty (single Euclidean distance from K=10 samples) actually\n      performs on par or marginally better than the 45D per-DOF features. This is\n      because in the worst case, the per-DOF features are near-spherical noise:\n      all 7 per-DOF conformal radii are nearly identical (ratio of max to min\n      radius ≈ 1.08), the covariance-derived features carry no cross-DOF signal,\n      and the 45D input dimensionality adds learnable parameters that overfit to\n      training augmentation patterns. The scalar distance, being lower-dimensional,\n      is more robust. The -0.2pp \"advantage\" of scalar over per-DOF is within\n      noise, but the direction is damning: the 42 additional per-DOF dimensions\n      provide zero marginal information. This directly contradicts the paper's core\n      insight that \"per-DOF conformal statistics encode which dimensions are\n      uncertain and how they co-vary.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 3: A4 Ablation — Per-DOF vs Scalar Uncertainty\"\n        rows:\n          - method: \"A3: no uncertainty\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 64.3\n            uncertainty_dim: 0\n          - method: \"A4: scalar (Euclidean)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 65.3\n            uncertainty_dim: 1\n          - method: \"ACR-VLA: per-DOF (45D)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 65.1\n            uncertainty_dim: 45\n          - method: \"Gap (per-DOF - scalar)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: \"-0.2\"\n            uncertainty_dim: \"—\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 4: Scatter plot of per-DOF conformal radii across 7 DOFs for 500 random steps. Near-circular cloud confirms ellipsoid collapse to sphere. Overlay: same plot from MVP 3D experiment showing clear elliptical structure.\"\n    analysis: >\n      CLAIM 1 IS DOUBLY KILLED. Not only do conformal features barely help over\n      no-uncertainty (A3), but per-DOF features don't beat scalar. The 45D→1D\n      compression loses nothing because there was nothing to lose: K=10 in 7-DOF\n      produces near-isotropic uncertainty estimates. The theoretical advantage of\n      multivariate CP (encoding cross-dimensional covariance) requires sufficient\n      samples relative to dimensionality, and K=10 with 7 DOFs is below the\n      practical threshold.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # plan_004: SRPO stacking\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"ACR-VLA stacked on SRPO\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_004\"\n    description: >\n      Train new CRM on SRPO-finetuned VLA checkpoint. Evaluate LIBERO-Plus.\n      Tests orthogonal complementarity claim.\n    expected_results:\n      srpo_baseline_liberoplus_sr_pct: 82.1\n      srpo_plus_acr_liberoplus_sr_pct: 83.4\n      srpo_stacking_gain_pp: 1.3\n      srpo_plus_acr_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 95.8\n      srpo_nominal_sr_pct: 97.2\n      srpo_nominal_degradation_pp: 1.4\n    result_rationale: >\n      SRPO at 82.1% has already corrected most of the failure modes that CRM's\n      generic residual learning can address. The remaining ~18% failures are\n      dominated by layout and language perturbations — distribution shifts that\n      (a) synthetic augmentation cannot cover, and (b) K=10 conformal statistics\n      cannot distinguish from in-distribution because flow-matching samples lack\n      diversity under these novel conditions. The 1.3pp stacking gain is below\n      the 2pp kill criterion. Moreover, the gain is not statistically robust:\n      SRPO's higher base performance means the failure cases are harder, more\n      diverse, and less amenable to a fixed residual correction pattern. The\n      1.4pp nominal degradation is proportionally less concerning (97.2→95.8)\n      but still present, suggesting CRM interferes with SRPO's already-good\n      action predictions on a nontrivial fraction of in-distribution steps.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 4: SRPO Stacking\"\n        rows:\n          - method: \"SRPO (training-time SOTA)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 82.1\n            libero_nominal_sr: 97.2\n          - method: \"SRPO + ACR-VLA\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 83.4\n            libero_nominal_sr: 95.8\n          - method: \"Stacking gain\"\n            liberoplus_sr: \"+1.3\"\n            libero_nominal_sr: \"-1.4\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 5: Per-perturbation stacking gain on top of SRPO. Only camera_noise and robot_state show >1pp gain; layout and language show 0 or negative.\"\n    analysis: >\n      CLAIM 3 IS KILLED. The 1.3pp stacking gain fails the 2pp criterion and is\n      likely not statistically significant across seeds. The orthogonal\n      complementarity argument is undermined: ACR-VLA's residual correction\n      overlaps with what SRPO already learned during training. The remaining\n      failure modes (layout, language) require semantic understanding beyond\n      what observation-conditioned residuals or conformal statistics can provide.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # plan_005: COC signal validation\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"COC Signal Validation\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_005\"\n    description: >\n      (a) Spearman ρ between COC coverage fraction and task success,\n      (b) ACI convergence, (c) ellipsoid volume comparison vs static.\n    expected_results:\n      coc_success_spearman_rho: 0.19\n      coc_success_spearman_p_value: 0.04\n      aci_convergence_episodes_to_stable: 45\n      aci_final_alpha_mean: 0.12\n      aci_final_alpha_std: 0.04\n      ellipsoid_volume_narrowing_pct: 6.2\n      static_calibration_volume_mean_log: 3.8\n      aci_adapted_volume_mean_log: 3.56\n    result_rationale: >\n      COC Spearman ρ = 0.19 is positive but below the 0.3 threshold needed for\n      a reliable ACI signal. The fundamental problem: COC measures consistency\n      between overlapping chunk predictions, but flow-matching VLAs generate\n      each chunk from a stochastic denoising process. At K=10, the inter-chunk\n      consistency is dominated by denoising noise rather than epistemic\n      uncertainty about the task. Two consecutive chunks may disagree not because\n      the policy is uncertain but because the diffusion sampling paths diverge.\n      This makes COC a noisy proxy for success — it detects catastrophic\n      failures (ρ > 0 because complete policy collapse does produce inconsistent\n      chunks) but cannot distinguish moderate uncertainty from sampling noise.\n      ACI converges (45 episodes) but converges to a threshold that produces\n      only 6.2% volume narrowing vs static — the adaptation is technically\n      functional but practically useless, as the conformal regions barely\n      change shape. The ellipsoid volume in log-space shifts from 3.8 to 3.56,\n      a negligible tightening that CRM cannot meaningfully exploit.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 5: COC Signal Quality\"\n        rows:\n          - metric: \"Spearman ρ (COC vs success)\"\n            value: 0.19\n            threshold: \"≥0.3\"\n            status: \"FAIL\"\n          - metric: \"Ellipsoid volume narrowing\"\n            value: \"6.2%\"\n            threshold: \"≥10%\"\n            status: \"FAIL\"\n          - metric: \"ACI convergence episodes\"\n            value: 45\n            threshold: \"—\"\n            status: \"—\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 6: Scatter plot of per-episode COC coverage fraction vs binary task success. Weak positive trend visible but high overlap between success/failure distributions. ρ = 0.19.\"\n      - \"Fig 7: ACI α_t trajectory over 100 episodes. Converges around episode 45 but oscillates ±0.04, indicating the COC signal is too noisy for stable adaptation.\"\n      - \"Fig 8: Ellipsoid volume (log scale) under static vs ACI-adapted calibration. Distributions largely overlap; ACI shifts the mean by 0.24 log-units (6.2% linear narrowing).\"\n    analysis: >\n      CLAIM 2 IS KILLED (both sub-criteria fail). COC is too noisy as an\n      ACI signal in the flow-matching setting. The self-referential per-step\n      coverage proxy — the key innovation enabling unsupervised online\n      adaptation — does not provide sufficient signal-to-noise ratio when\n      the base policy's stochasticity dominates inter-chunk disagreement.\n      The ACI mechanism is mathematically sound (it does converge) but\n      converges to a near-static threshold because the noisy COC signal\n      averages out to roughly the marginal coverage rate.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # plan_006: Ablation A1 — CRM without ACI\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"Ablation A1 — CRM without ACI (static calibration)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_006\"\n    description: >\n      CRM with static CGS calibration, no online ACI adaptation.\n      Isolates ACI contribution.\n    expected_results:\n      a1_liberoplus_sr_pct: 64.7\n      full_acr_vla_liberoplus_sr_pct: 65.1\n      a1_gap_pp: 0.4\n      a1_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 93.9\n    result_rationale: >\n      ACI's contribution is negligible (0.4pp) because, as plan_005 shows, the\n      COC-driven adaptation produces only 6.2% volume narrowing. The CRM input\n      features under static vs ACI-adapted calibration are nearly identical —\n      the conformal radii shift by <7% on average, well within the noise floor\n      that CRM has already learned to ignore. This confirms that in the worst\n      case, ACI is a dead module: it runs, it converges, but the adapted\n      conformal regions are functionally identical to static ones. The entire\n      online adaptation pathway (COC → ACI → updated conformal threshold →\n      modified CGS features → CRM) is a no-op in practice.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 6: A1 Ablation — ACI Contribution\"\n        rows:\n          - method: \"A1: CRM + static CGS\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 64.7\n            nominal_sr: 93.9\n          - method: \"ACR-VLA (full, with ACI)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 65.1\n            nominal_sr: 93.7\n          - method: \"ACI contribution\"\n            liberoplus_sr: \"+0.4\"\n            nominal_sr: \"-0.2\"\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      ACI adds nothing. The entire online adaptation subsystem — arguably the\n      most theoretically elegant component — is inert because its input signal\n      (COC) is too noisy and its output effect (volume narrowing) is too small.\n      This eliminates Claim 2's practical value entirely.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # plan_007: Ablation A2 — ACI without CRM\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"Ablation A2 — ACI without CRM (sample selection only)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_007\"\n    description: >\n      CGS + COC + sample selection only, extending ReconVLA to multivariate.\n      No learned residual correction. Isolates CRM contribution.\n    expected_results:\n      a2_liberoplus_sr_pct: 62.9\n      full_acr_vla_liberoplus_sr_pct: 65.1\n      a2_vs_base_gain_pp: 0.9\n      a2_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 96.1\n      reconvla_scalar_selection_liberoplus_sr_pct: 63.2\n    result_rationale: >\n      Without CRM, the system reduces to \"multivariate ReconVLA\" — selecting\n      among K=10 samples based on conformal region membership rather than\n      Euclidean distance. The 0.9pp gain over base is marginal and actually\n      slightly worse than scalar ReconVLA-style selection (63.2%), because the\n      near-spherical conformal regions at K=10 in 7-DOF make the multivariate\n      selection criterion equivalent to (but noisier than) simple Euclidean\n      distance. The selection approach is fundamentally limited: it can only\n      choose from K=10 existing samples, and at this small K the best sample\n      is often close to the mean regardless of selection criterion. CRM's\n      ability to synthesize a correction beyond any individual sample is what\n      drives the ~3pp full pipeline gain — confirming that the residual\n      learning, not the conformal machinery, is the active ingredient.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 7: A2 Ablation — CRM vs Sample Selection\"\n        rows:\n          - method: \"SmolVLA (base)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 62.0\n          - method: \"A2: multivariate selection (no CRM)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 62.9\n          - method: \"ReconVLA-style scalar selection\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 63.2\n          - method: \"ACR-VLA (full)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 65.1\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 9: CDF of action error improvement. Sample selection (A2) shifts CDF marginally; CRM (full) produces a larger but still modest shift. The CRM shift is similar to A3 (no conformal), confirming the residual mechanism.\"\n    analysis: >\n      CRM is doing the heavy lifting (65.1 vs 62.9 without it). But as A3\n      shows, CRM doesn't need conformal features. The multivariate sample\n      selection is slightly worse than scalar selection, a particularly\n      damning result for the paper's core thesis.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # plan_008: Per-perturbation breakdown\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"Per-Perturbation Breakdown\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_008\"\n    description: >\n      ACR-VLA gain across 7 LIBERO-Plus perturbation dimensions.\n    expected_results:\n      per_perturbation_gain_pp:\n        camera_noise: 4.8\n        gaussian_noise: 4.2\n        robot_state_offset: 3.5\n        lighting_change: 2.1\n        background_change: 1.5\n        layout_change: 0.3\n        language_rephrase: -0.2\n      avg_augmentable_gain_pp: 4.17\n      avg_non_augmentable_gain_pp: 0.53\n    result_rationale: >\n      The gain pattern exposes the mechanism: ACR-VLA helps most on perturbation\n      types covered by synthetic augmentation (camera noise, Gaussian noise,\n      robot-state offset — avg 4.17pp). These are precisely the perturbations\n      where the CRM's observation-conditioned residual has seen training examples.\n      Non-augmentable perturbations (lighting, background, layout, language)\n      show minimal or zero gain because (a) the residual network has no training\n      signal for these shifts, and (b) K=10 conformal statistics are equally\n      uninformative for novel vs familiar perturbations (the near-spherical\n      uncertainty regions don't differentially respond to distribution shift\n      type). Language rephrase shows -0.2pp because the CRM occasionally\n      applies a correction to steps where the base VLA would have been correct\n      under the rephrased instruction, and the conformal features provide no\n      signal to suppress this spurious correction. The 4-5pp gains on\n      augmentable perturbations are genuine but attributable to residual\n      learning (see A3), not conformal features.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 8: Per-Perturbation ACR-VLA Gain\"\n        rows:\n          - perturbation: \"Camera noise\"\n            base_sr: 55.2\n            acr_sr: 60.0\n            gain_pp: \"+4.8\"\n          - perturbation: \"Gaussian noise\"\n            base_sr: 58.1\n            acr_sr: 62.3\n            gain_pp: \"+4.2\"\n          - perturbation: \"Robot-state offset\"\n            base_sr: 51.8\n            acr_sr: 55.3\n            gain_pp: \"+3.5\"\n          - perturbation: \"Lighting\"\n            base_sr: 68.3\n            acr_sr: 70.4\n            gain_pp: \"+2.1\"\n          - perturbation: \"Background\"\n            base_sr: 71.0\n            acr_sr: 72.5\n            gain_pp: \"+1.5\"\n          - perturbation: \"Layout\"\n            base_sr: 60.5\n            acr_sr: 60.8\n            gain_pp: \"+0.3\"\n          - perturbation: \"Language\"\n            base_sr: 69.1\n            acr_sr: 68.9\n            gain_pp: \"-0.2\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 10: Grouped bar chart of per-perturbation gain. Clear divide between augmentable (green, >3pp) and non-augmentable (gray, <2pp) perturbation types. Annotated with A3 per-perturbation comparison showing near-identical pattern.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The perturbation breakdown is the most informative diagnostic. It reveals\n      that ACR-VLA's gains are entirely driven by synthetic augmentation coverage,\n      not by conformal uncertainty adaptation. A truly uncertainty-aware system\n      should help on novel perturbations (where uncertainty is highest) — instead,\n      gains correlate with augmentation coverage (r > 0.9). This is the signature\n      of a memorized residual, not an adaptive uncertainty-conditioned correction.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # plan_009: Ablation A5 — CGS vs Bonferroni\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"Ablation A5 — CGS vs per-DOF Bonferroni\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_009\"\n    description: >\n      Joint Mahalanobis (CGS) vs per-DOF Bonferroni correction.\n      Tests whether multivariate CP outperforms independent marginal CP.\n    expected_results:\n      a5_cgs_liberoplus_sr_pct: 65.1\n      a5_bonferroni_liberoplus_sr_pct: 65.0\n      a5_gap_pp: 0.1\n      bonferroni_ellipsoid_volume_ratio_vs_cgs: 1.03\n    result_rationale: >\n      Since the K=10 Ledoit-Wolf covariance is 71% identity, CGS's joint\n      Mahalanobis ellipsoid is nearly a sphere — functionally equivalent to\n      independent per-DOF intervals with Bonferroni correction. The 0.1pp\n      difference is pure noise. The theoretical advantage of CGS (capturing\n      cross-DOF covariance to produce tighter ellipsoids aligned with the\n      actual uncertainty structure) requires off-diagonal covariance elements\n      that are statistically significant, which K=10 in 7-DOF cannot provide.\n      The Bonferroni rectangular region and CGS near-spherical region have\n      similar volumes (ratio 1.03) and contain essentially the same samples,\n      producing identical CRM inputs in practice.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 9: A5 Ablation — CGS vs Bonferroni\"\n        rows:\n          - method: \"CGS (joint Mahalanobis)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 65.1\n            volume_log: 3.56\n          - method: \"Per-DOF Bonferroni\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 65.0\n            volume_log: 3.67\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 11: 2D projection (DOF 1 vs DOF 2) of CGS ellipsoid vs Bonferroni rectangle for 100 random steps. Ellipsoid is visually circular, nearly inscribed in the rectangle. Cross-DOF tilt angle < 3° from axes.\"\n    analysis: >\n      CGS's multivariate CP offers no advantage over naïve Bonferroni. This is\n      a direct consequence of the covariance collapse at K=10. In the MVP 3D\n      setting, CGS showed clear elliptical tilt (15-25° off-axis), demonstrating\n      genuine cross-DOF structure. At 7-DOF with K=10, this structure vanishes.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # plan_010: Ablation A6 — COC vs episode-level ACI\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"Ablation A6 — COC vs episode-level task success for ACI\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_010\"\n    description: >\n      ACI updated with episode-level binary success signal instead of\n      per-step COC. Tests self-referential signal value.\n    expected_results:\n      a6_episode_aci_liberoplus_sr_pct: 65.0\n      coc_aci_liberoplus_sr_pct: 65.1\n      a6_gap_pp: -0.1\n      episode_aci_convergence_episodes: 30\n      episode_aci_volume_narrowing_pct: 7.8\n    result_rationale: >\n      Episode-level ACI actually converges faster (30 vs 45 episodes) and\n      produces slightly more volume narrowing (7.8% vs 6.2%) because the\n      binary success signal, while sparser, is lower-noise than COC. COC's\n      per-step granularity is theoretically valuable but in practice the\n      noise from flow-matching stochasticity drowns the signal advantage.\n      The -0.1pp \"gap\" (episode ACI marginally better) further undermines\n      Claim 2: COC's self-referential per-step mechanism — the paper's\n      claimed innovation for unsupervised online adaptation — performs no\n      better than the simpler episode-level signal. Neither signal produces\n      meaningful ACI adaptation at this operating point, so the comparison\n      is between two flavors of ineffective.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 10: A6 Ablation — COC vs Episode-level ACI\"\n        rows:\n          - signal: \"COC (per-step, self-referential)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 65.1\n            convergence_episodes: 45\n            volume_narrowing_pct: 6.2\n          - signal: \"Episode success (binary)\"\n            liberoplus_sr: 65.0\n            convergence_episodes: 30\n            volume_narrowing_pct: 7.8\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 12: Overlay of ACI α_t trajectories for COC-driven vs episode-driven adaptation. Episode signal is smoother, converges earlier, but both plateau at similar α values.\"\n    analysis: >\n      COC provides no advantage over episode-level signal. The per-step\n      self-referential innovation adds complexity without benefit. For the\n      reframed paper, this is a useful negative result: it characterizes\n      the signal-to-noise boundary where per-step conformal consistency\n      breaks down.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # plan_011: Ablation A7 — DiU aleatoric vs CGS epistemic\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"Ablation A7 — DiU aleatoric vs CGS epistemic features\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_011\"\n    description: >\n      CRM with DiU aleatoric uncertainty features (7D) replacing CGS\n      epistemic features (45D). Also test combined 52D input.\n    expected_results:\n      a7_diu_only_liberoplus_sr_pct: 64.9\n      a7_combined_52d_liberoplus_sr_pct: 65.4\n      full_cgs_only_liberoplus_sr_pct: 65.1\n      a3_no_uncertainty_liberoplus_sr_pct: 64.3\n      diu_vs_cgs_gap_pp: -0.2\n      combined_vs_cgs_gap_pp: 0.3\n    result_rationale: >\n      DiU's 7D aleatoric features (per-DOF variance from single-pass\n      estimation) perform comparably to CGS's 45D epistemic features\n      (64.9 vs 65.1, gap -0.2pp within noise). This is because in the\n      worst case, both are providing low-quality uncertainty signals: CGS\n      is near-spherical noise, and DiU captures aleatoric variance that\n      is present in-distribution and out-of-distribution alike (not\n      discriminative for perturbation detection). The combined 52D input\n      shows a marginal 0.3pp improvement, which is likely from the slight\n      orthogonal information in DiU's per-sample variance vs CGS's\n      inter-sample spread, but is not significant. The key takeaway: when\n      the CGS features are degraded (as in worst case), replacing them\n      with a simpler uncertainty source makes no difference because the\n      CRM is already ignoring uncertainty features in favor of observation\n      conditioning. All uncertainty variants cluster within 1.1pp of each\n      other, while all are only ~1pp above the no-uncertainty A3 baseline.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 11: A7 Ablation — Uncertainty Feature Source\"\n        rows:\n          - features: \"None (A3)\"\n            dim: 0\n            liberoplus_sr: 64.3\n          - features: \"DiU aleatoric\"\n            dim: 7\n            liberoplus_sr: 64.9\n          - features: \"CGS epistemic (full)\"\n            dim: 45\n            liberoplus_sr: 65.1\n          - features: \"Combined\"\n            dim: 52\n            liberoplus_sr: 65.4\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 13: Feature ablation ladder. All uncertainty variants within 1.1pp band; observation-conditioned residual (A3 at 64.3) provides the bulk of improvement over base (62.0).\"\n    analysis: >\n      The epistemic-vs-aleatoric distinction is moot when both signals are\n      degraded. CGS's theoretical advantage (epistemic uncertainty rises\n      under distribution shift) is lost because the covariance estimation\n      noise masks the epistemic signal. DiU's aleatoric features are at\n      least lower-dimensional and don't carry covariance estimation noise,\n      making them a simpler alternative that performs identically.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # plan_012: Feature importance analysis\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"CRM Feature Importance Analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_012\"\n    description: >\n      Gradient magnitude of CRM output w.r.t. 45 uncertainty features,\n      broken down by perturbation type.\n    expected_results:\n      avg_gradient_magnitude_observation_features: 0.042\n      avg_gradient_magnitude_conformal_features: 0.008\n      observation_to_conformal_gradient_ratio: 5.25\n      top5_features_by_gradient: [\"obs_embed_dim_127\", \"obs_embed_dim_43\", \"obs_embed_dim_201\", \"base_action_dof_1\", \"base_action_dof_3\"]\n      conformal_feature_gradient_std_across_perturbations: 0.002\n      max_conformal_feature_gradient_any_perturbation: 0.014\n    result_rationale: >\n      Gradient analysis reveals that CRM overwhelmingly attends to observation\n      embedding dimensions (avg gradient magnitude 0.042) over conformal\n      features (0.008) — a 5.25× ratio. The top 5 features by gradient\n      magnitude are all observation embedding or base action dimensions; no\n      conformal feature appears in the top 20. Furthermore, conformal feature\n      gradients show near-zero variance across perturbation types (std 0.002),\n      meaning CRM does not differentially use conformal features for different\n      perturbation types — it has learned to effectively ignore them. The\n      maximum gradient magnitude for any conformal feature under any\n      perturbation (0.014) is still 3× lower than the average observation\n      feature gradient. This is the mechanistic explanation for A3's result:\n      CRM's learned weights suppress the noisy conformal features and rely\n      on observation conditioning for correction decisions.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 12: Feature Importance — Gradient Magnitude\"\n        rows:\n          - feature_group: \"Observation embedding (128D)\"\n            avg_gradient: 0.042\n            pct_of_total: \"78.5%\"\n          - feature_group: \"Base action (7D)\"\n            avg_gradient: 0.031\n            pct_of_total: \"14.2%\"\n          - feature_group: \"Conformal per-DOF std (7D)\"\n            avg_gradient: 0.009\n            pct_of_total: \"3.1%\"\n          - feature_group: \"Conformal radii (7D)\"\n            avg_gradient: 0.008\n            pct_of_total: \"2.8%\"\n          - feature_group: \"Ellipsoid volume (1D)\"\n            avg_gradient: 0.007\n            pct_of_total: \"0.4%\"\n          - feature_group: \"Cross-DOF covariance (21D + 2D)\"\n            avg_gradient: 0.006\n            pct_of_total: \"1.0%\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 14: Heatmap of gradient magnitudes (45 conformal features × 7 perturbation types). Near-uniform low magnitude across all cells — no perturbation-specific conformal feature usage pattern.\"\n      - \"Fig 15: Bar chart comparing observation vs conformal feature gradient magnitudes. 5.25× ratio clearly visible.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The gradient analysis provides the mechanistic autopsy of Claim 1's\n      failure. CRM learned to ignore conformal features because they are\n      uninformative (near-spherical, perturbation-agnostic). The network\n      allocated its limited capacity to observation features, which do carry\n      perturbation-discriminative information. This is rational behavior by\n      the optimizer — it confirms the features are genuinely useless, not\n      that the architecture is wrong.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # plan_013: SnapFlow integration\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"SnapFlow K=10 1-step Distilled Generation\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_013\"\n    description: >\n      K=10 samples via 1-step distilled flow matching (SnapFlow).\n      Test sample diversity and inference speed.\n    expected_results:\n      snapflow_inference_time_ms: 12\n      standard_10step_inference_time_ms: 38\n      speedup_factor: 3.17\n      snapflow_sample_diversity_avg_std_per_dof: 0.018\n      standard_sample_diversity_avg_std_per_dof: 0.031\n      diversity_reduction_pct: 42\n      snapflow_acr_liberoplus_sr_pct: 63.8\n      standard_acr_liberoplus_sr_pct: 65.1\n      snapflow_degradation_pp: 1.3\n    result_rationale: >\n      SnapFlow's 1-step distilled generation produces K=10 samples 3.17×\n      faster but with 42% less diversity (per-DOF std drops from 0.031 to\n      0.018). The diversity reduction directly impacts conformal statistics:\n      the already-noisy K=10 covariance becomes even more concentrated,\n      with shrinkage intensity increasing from 0.71 to ~0.82. The 1.3pp\n      performance drop (65.1→63.8) is modest because, as established,\n      conformal features contribute little — the degradation comes from\n      reduced sample diversity slightly weakening the mean-action estimate\n      that CRM uses as its base. The 3.17× speedup is the only unambiguous\n      positive result, but it's a SnapFlow contribution, not an ACR-VLA\n      contribution. For the reframed paper, this could be cited as evidence\n      that the pipeline is practical at inference time, but it doesn't save\n      the conformal features story.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 13: SnapFlow Integration\"\n        rows:\n          - method: \"ACR-VLA (10-step sampling)\"\n            inference_ms: 38\n            liberoplus_sr: 65.1\n            sample_diversity: 0.031\n          - method: \"ACR-VLA + SnapFlow (1-step)\"\n            inference_ms: 12\n            liberoplus_sr: 63.8\n            sample_diversity: 0.018\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 16: Sample diversity (per-DOF std) histogram for 10-step vs SnapFlow 1-step. SnapFlow distribution is sharply concentrated near zero, further compressing the covariance estimate.\"\n    analysis: >\n      SnapFlow trades diversity for speed. In the worst case, this further\n      degrades already-insufficient conformal features. The practical\n      takeaway: if conformal features are noise anyway, SnapFlow's speedup\n      is free — the system loses nothing by using faster, less diverse\n      samples because the conformal pathway was already inert.\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"1e6879ea-cc71-427e-97a6-96f2664b189b","total_cost_usd":0.37640025,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11303,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"output_tokens":11737,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11303,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":11737,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11303,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11303},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":6261,"outputTokens":28,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.0064010000000000004,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":11737,"cacheReadInputTokens":11841,"cacheCreationInputTokens":11303,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.36999925,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"393904e3-7b02-4baf-96a6-bff1e1f87066"}
